TITLE:: Tunnel
summary:: a stereo delay with a band filter in the loop, modulation, ducking, tape character and a small reverb, as a UGen and as an object, with a window
categories:: Libraries>Tunnel
related:: Classes/FxParamUnit, Classes/TunnelView, Classes/FxUnit, Classes/FxChain

DESCRIPTION::
A stereo delay with a character section - time or tempo, a band filter in the loop, modulation, gate, ducking, tape noise and wobble, and a reverb before, after or inside it - in the spirit of Ableton's Echo, running anywhere a UGen does, with a window.

code::
Ndef(\keys, { Tunnel.ar(Blip.ar([220, 221], 8) * Decay2.kr(Impulse.kr(1), 0.01, 0.2) * 0.3, \tunnel) }).play;

t = Tunnel(Ndef(\keys), \tunnel);
t.gui;
t.preset = 'worn tape';
t.sync = true; t.bpm = 128; t.divL = '1/8d'; t.divR = '1/4';
t.feedback = 0.7; t.cross = 1;      // ping pong
::

Two lines, left and right, up to 4 seconds each. Free, they read code::timeL:: and code::timeR::. With code::sync:: on, they read a division of code::bpm::; nothing here follows a clock of its own, so you say the tempo. code::link:: makes the right follow the left, and code::offset:: moves it a little early or late. code::lineTime:: gives you the time in seconds, and the display draws from that.

Feedback goes through a band filter, a high pass and a low pass moved together by code::filterFreq:: and code::filterWidth::. It is saturated on the way back in, so past 100% it swells and holds rather than running away. code::cross:: sends each side's feedback to the other: at 100% it is a ping pong.

Changing the time does one of two things. With code::repitch::, the read head glides and the echoes bend, as on a tape machine. Without it, two read heads crossfade, and the echoes already in the line stay at their pitch.

The lfo, or the input's envelope by way of code::envMix::, moves the time and the filter. code::gateThr:: keeps quiet input out of the lines, code::duckThr:: makes the echoes get out of the way of the input, code::noise:: puts hiss in the loop and code::wobble:: makes the tape unsteady. code::reverb:: can sit before the lines, after them, or inside the feedback.

Hiss in the loop never goes silent, so in a SuperDirt insert an orbit with code::noise:: above 0 does not sleep.

subsection:: The object and the UGen
It is an link::Classes/FxParamUnit::, so everything that class documents works here: code::Tunnel.ar(in, name)::, code::Tunnel(node, name)::, code::Tunnel.onBus(bus, numChannels)::, presets, the clipboard, code::gui::. Its window's display is a link::Classes/TunnelView::.

subsection:: The window
code::gui:: opens a device (link::Classes/TunnelPanel::) on four pages - echo, modulation, character, space - under one display, a link::Classes/TunnelView::: the echoes of a hit along a time axis, the left line above and the right below, and the loop filter they go through. Drag an echo along for its line's time and away from the middle for feedback; drag the band or one of its edges. With code::sync:: on, each line's knob is a stepped knob over the divisions; code::tap:: is a button there. What does nothing as things are set is dimmed (code::*isActive::). The echoes, the name and the knobs that place them are the delay family's teal (code::*identityColour::).

subsection:: Parameters
Each is a property of the object: read it, set it. The numbers are in the order of the node's control, which is what saved node maps carry. Internal ones without a knob, if any, are described above.

table::
## strong::parameter:: || strong::range:: || strong::default:: || strong::what it does::
## code::sync:: || on off || false || in time with the bpm, by division - off, in milliseconds
## code::timeL:: || 0.001 .. 4 s || 0.375 || the left line's time - or drag its echoes along in the display
## code::timeR:: || 0.001 .. 4 s || 0.5 || the right line's time - or drag its echoes along in the display
## code::divL:: || 1/32 1/16t 1/16 1/16d 1/8t 1/8 1/8d 1/4t 1/4 1/4d 1/2 1/2d 1/1 || 1/8d || the left line's time in sync: a division of the bpm, t for triplet, d for dotted - or drag its echoes along
## code::divR:: || 1/32 1/16t 1/16 1/16d 1/8t 1/8 1/8d 1/4t 1/4 1/4d 1/2 1/2d 1/1 || 1/4 || the right line's time in sync: a division of the bpm, t for triplet, d for dotted - or drag its echoes along
## code::link:: || on off || false || the right line follows the left - swing still moves it
## code::offset:: || -0.33 .. 0.33 || 0 || the right line a little early or late against the left
## code::bpm:: || 20 .. 300 bpm || 120 || the tempo the divisions are counted in - type it, or tap it
## code::input:: || -36 .. 12 dB || 0 || the level into the lines: louder echoes, and past 0 dB they saturate
## code::feedback:: || 0 .. 1.2 || 0.5 || how much goes round again - saturated, so past 100 it swells and holds; or drag the echoes away from the middle
## code::cross:: || 0 .. 1 || 0 || how much of each side's feedback goes to the other: at 100 the echoes swap sides every time - or the wheel on the echoes
## code::invert:: || on off || false || turn the feedback's polarity over: hollow, at short times
## code::filterOn:: || on off || true || the band filter in the loop: every echo goes through it once more, so they darken as they go
## code::filterFreq:: || 40 .. 16000 Hz || 1600 || the middle of the band - or drag the band along in the display
## code::filterWidth:: || 0.2 .. 9 oct || 5 || how wide the band is, in octaves - or the wheel on the band
## code::lfoShape:: || sine triangle sawUp sawDown square sampleHold noise || sine || the lfo's shape
## code::lfoRate:: || 0.01 .. 20 Hz || 0.5 || the lfo's speed, when it runs free
## code::lfoPhase:: || 0 .. 1 || 0.25 || how far apart the two sides' lfos are: 50 is opposite
## code::modTime:: || 0 .. 1 || 0 || how much the lfo moves the delay time, up to half of it each way: a chorus at short times, a warble at long ones
## code::modFilter:: || 0 .. 1 || 0 || how much the lfo moves the band, up to two octaves each way
## code::envMix:: || 0 .. 1 || 0 || lfo or envelope: at 100 the input's level does the moving
## code::gateThr:: || -96 .. 0 dB || -96 || what falls under this does not go into the lines
## code::gateRel:: || 0.005 .. 2 s || 0.2 || how quickly the gate closes
## code::duckThr:: || -60 .. 0 dB || 0 || the echoes get out of the way while the input is over this - at 0 dB they never do
## code::duckRel:: || 0.01 .. 2 s || 0.25 || how quickly they come back
## code::noise:: || 0 .. 1 || 0 || tape hiss in the loop - it never goes quiet
## code::noiseMorph:: || 0 .. 1 || 0.5 || the hiss from dark to bright
## code::wobble:: || 0 .. 1 || 0 || an unsteady tape speed
## code::wobbleMorph:: || 0 .. 1 || 0.3 || the wobble from a slow drift to a fast flutter
## code::repitch:: || on off || false || a change of time bends the echoes, like a tape; off, they crossfade
## code::reverb:: || 0 .. 1 || 0 || how much of the small reverb
## code::reverbDecay:: || 0 .. 1 || 0.6 || how big it is
## code::reverbPos:: || pre post loop || post || the reverb before the lines, after them, or inside the feedback, where it builds
## code::width:: || 0 .. 2 || 1 || how far apart the echoes sit: 0 is mono, past 100 wider than they came in
## code::mix:: || 0 .. 1 || 0.35 || how much of the effect goes out, against the dry signal
## code::output:: || -36 .. 24 dB || 0 || the level of the whole thing, in dB
## code::lfoSync:: || free four two one half quarter eighth sixteenth third sixth twelfth || free || the lfo free, at its rate - or one period every so many cycles of Tidal's clock
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: divisionNames
the divisions by name.

METHOD:: divisionBeats
how many beats each lasts.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: lineTime
a line's time in seconds as the settings have it, before any lfo: side 0 is the left, 1 the right.
ARGUMENT:: side

METHOD:: tap
Tap the beat: from the second tap on, the time between taps (the mean of the last four) is a beat. It sets code::bpm::; free, it sets the left time to the beat too, and the right keeps its ratio to the left. A pause of two seconds starts over.
ARGUMENT:: at
the time of the tap, in seconds; code::Main.elapsedTime:: when nil.
RETURNS:: the beat in seconds, or nil after the first tap.

METHOD:: divisionNear
the division nearest a time at the bpm, by ratio.
ARGUMENT:: seconds

METHOD:: echoes
Every echo of one hit into both lines, as the display draws them: code::[side, time, gain, passes]::, side 0 the left and 1 the right, the gain against the hit (negative when it comes back upside down). A pass through a line is its time, the band once and the feedback; code::cross:: sends that share to the other side; the loop's saturation is a clip at 1. No lfo, gate, duck or noise: the settings' arithmetic only.
ARGUMENT:: kL
a factor on the left time (where the lfo has it).
ARGUMENT:: kR
a factor on the right time.
ARGUMENT:: floor
the quietest kept, in dB.
ARGUMENT:: maxPasses
how many trips round the loop at most.
ARGUMENT:: mono
one line, which cross cannot reach.
ARGUMENT:: until
the latest time kept, in seconds.
code::
t = Tunnel.new; t.filterOn = false;
t.echoes.keep(4).postln;    // [ [ 0, 0.375, 1.0, 1 ], [ 1, 0.5, 1.0, 1 ], [ 1, 1.0, 0.5, 2 ], [ 0, 0.75, 0.5, 2 ] ]
::

METHOD:: repeats
how many echoes are heard before they fall under a floor in dB (default -48): code::inf:: when the loop holds.
ARGUMENT:: floor

METHOD:: loopGain
what one trip round the loop keeps: the feedback and the band once.

METHOD:: bandGain
what one pass through the band keeps, at its middle.

METHOD:: hiPass
the bottom of the band, in Hz.

METHOD:: loPass
the top of the band, in Hz.

METHOD:: filterMag
the band's gain at some frequencies, as a ratio, from the same biquad arithmetic the server runs.
ARGUMENT:: freqs
ARGUMENT:: sr

EXAMPLES::

code::
s.boot;
Ndef(\keys, { Tunnel.ar(Blip.ar([220, 221], 8) * Decay2.kr(Impulse.kr(1), 0.01, 0.2) * 0.3, \tunnel) }).play;
t = Tunnel(Ndef(\keys), \tunnel);
t.gui;
t.preset = Tunnel.presetNames.choose;
t.free; Ndef(\keys).clear;
::
